{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n60 \n \n \n \nThis sequence of preliminary monochrome flame images is a life history of an example Flame Design \nflame. \n(a) Shortly after ignition, the ignition-created soot is being carried out of the flame by the gas flow from \nthe porous spherical burner. \n(b) The amount of soot has decreased dramatically and the blue flame (neglecting the bright spots of the \nsoot particulate) has expanded and dimmed. Since the fuel is inside the flame and oxygen is outside, the \nflame expands outward until it reaches enough oxygen to consume the fuel. The gas flow is outward from \nthe burner sphere, so the oxygen must diffuse inward due to its concentration gradient. That gradient, \nand thus the oxygen transport, diminishes (i.e., flattens) with time due to both the oxygen consumption \nand the release of combustion byproducts like carbon dioxide and water vapor. A flame becomes \neffectively blanketed by these byproducts, inhibiting the oxygen transport. \n(c) The visible soot has mostly disappeared, and the flame has further grown and dimmed. The base of \nthe flame has retreated from the burner tube because of heat loss. \n(d) The fiber array has begun translating with the first ceramic fiber at the edge of flame, where its glow \nis visible at the top of the image. Again, the soot continued to disappear, and the flame continued to grow \nand retreat from the burner tube. \n(e) Two fibers are in the flame and the third is close enough to visibly glow. The flame is a shell, where the \nfibers are cooler (i.e., dimmer in this gray-scale image) within the flame. A comparison of images (d) and \n(e) reveals that the flame base is oscillating, which is a sign of approaching extinction. \n(f) The flame has nearly extinguished due to radiative heat loss and is very faint in comparison to the \nearlier images. The fibers are dimmer in image (f) than (e), indicating the decrease in flame temperature.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 60}}